{"causes":["The fragmentation of the Western Roman Empire into smaller kingdoms created a power vacuum that would shape European politics for centuries to come."],"effects":["The feudal system established during this period persisted in various forms until the Industrial Revolution, influencing social hierarchies and economic systems."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"western-roman-empire","sourceName":"Western Roman Empire","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"middle-ages","targetName":"Middle Ages","context":"The decline of the Western Roman Empire led to the fragmentation into smaller kingdoms, creating a power vacuum that would shape European politics."},{"sourceSlug":"carolingian-renaissance","sourceName":"Carolingian Renaissance","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"middle-ages","targetName":"Middle Ages","context":"The Carolingian Renaissance brought about a renewed interest in classical learning, leading to significant advancements in art, literature, and science."},{"sourceSlug":"black-death","sourceName":"Black Death","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"europe","targetName":"Europe","context":"The devastating pandemic swept through Europe, killing an estimated 60% of the population and leaving a lasting impact on social and economic structures."},{"sourceSlug":"bayeux-tapestry","sourceName":"Bayeux Tapestry","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"middle-ages","targetName":"Middle Ages","context":"The iconic tapestry, measuring over 230 feet long, is a testament to the artistic skill and storytelling prowess of medieval artisans."},{"sourceSlug":"university-of-oxford","sourceName":"University of Oxford","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"middle-ages","targetName":"Middle Ages","context":"The University of Oxford emerged during this period, becoming a center of learning and intellectual inquiry that would shape the course of Western civilization."},{"sourceSlug":"university-of-cambridge","sourceName":"University of Cambridge","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"middle-ages","targetName":"Middle Ages","context":"The University of Cambridge also emerged during this period, contributing to the development of medieval learning and intellectual traditions."},{"sourceSlug":"feudal-system","sourceName":"Feudal System","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"middle-ages","targetName":"Middle Ages","context":"The feudal system established during this period persisted in various forms until the Industrial Revolution, influencing social hierarchies and economic systems."}],"places":[{"name":"Europe","role":"Major battle"},{"name":"Bayeux","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Paris","role":"Capital"}],"texts":[],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q12554","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_Ages"],"tags":["historical period","Prehistoric","Classical & Ancient Periods","periodization","political-history"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Middle Ages is remembered as a landmark timeframe of the Classical era, referenced in 198 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Defined subsequent political and social structures.","summary":"The Middle Ages (also Medieval Period or Medieval Era) is the historiographical term for the central period of European history, conventionally dated from approximately 500 CE (the collapse of the Western Roman Empire in 476 CE or the death of Boethius in 524 CE) to approximately 1500 CE (the expulsion of the Moors from Spain and Columbus's voyage to the Americas in 1492, or Luther's Reformation in 1517). The term was coined during the Italian Renaissance — first implied by humanists like Flavio Biondo and Petrarch as a way of distinguishing the 'middle' period of history between the grandeur of classical antiquity and the recovery of that grandeur in their own era from both bookends. The very concept thus embeds a value judgment: the Middle Ages were, from the Renaissance humanist perspective, an unfortunate interval that interrupted the superior civilisation of ancient Rome.

The Middle Ages encompass enormous diversity — geographically (European, Byzantine, Islamic, Chinese, and other civilisations all have their 'medieval' periods, though the European usage is most conventional), chronologically (the 'Early Middle Ages' c. 500–1000 CE and 'High/Late Middle Ages' c. 1000–1500 CE are often distinguished), and culturally (the period spans from the Benedictine monasteries of early medieval Europe to the Gothic cathedrals, scholastic universities, Black Death, and early capitalism of the later medieval period). The conventional division into Early, High, and Late Middle Ages reflects real differences in European historical experience: depopulation and cultural contraction in the early period, demographic and economic expansion in the High Middle Ages, and the crises (Black Death, Great Schism, Hundred Years' War) and transformations of the later period.

The reputation of the Middle Ages has been profoundly shaped by historiographical tradition. The Renaissance humanist dismissal (the term 'Dark Ages' for the early medieval period) has been gradually revised by modern medieval historians who have demonstrated the period's significant intellectual, artistic, architectural, and institutional achievements — the preservation of classical learning in Irish and Carolingian monasteries, the construction of the great cathedrals, the invention of the university, the development of common law, and the agricultural and commercial revolution of the High Middle Ages.","subjects":["Medieval History","European History","Medieval Era","Historiography","World History","Classical Era","Periodisation","Historical Framework"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT","WORLD_SYSTEMS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Middle Ages played a significant role in shaping European history, laying the groundwork for the Renaissance and influencing social hierarchies and economic systems. The period also saw significant scientific discoveries and cultural achievements.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
